  (c) VERDICT ON SAMPLE SIZE vs EFFECT SIZE
  A detector that warns on 0 of 6 onsets warns on ~0 of 15 and ~0 of
  20 unless its per-event warn probability is actually non-zero.
  Sample size governs how PRECISELY a null result is stated; only
  effect size governs whether the result is null. At a warned rate
  of 0.00 against a 0.14 no-skill rate, the binding constraint is
  EFFECT SIZE, unambiguously. More onsets would sharpen the negative
  finding — they could not plausibly convert 0/6 into a detection.

